# South Asia batch (1498-2021): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"as14984": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese King Manuel I sent navigator Vasco da Gama to find a sea route to India and reach the spice trade direct, cutting out the Muslim and Venetian middlemen. Da Gama reached Calicut in May 1498 and returned with pepper worth many times the cost of the voyage, having lost about half his crew. A direct sea link between Europe and Asia now existed, and Portugal used it to impose armed trade on an ocean no single power had policed.",
 now="The sea route made the Indian Ocean a field for European naval power for 450 years and began the pattern of armed trading companies that ended in the conquest of India. The ocean is again a zone of naval competition among India, China and the United States.",
 story="Portuguese navigator Vasco da Gama sailed from Lisbon in July 1497 with four ships, rounded the Cape of Good Hope and, with a pilot hired at Malindi on the East African coast, crossed the Indian Ocean to Calicut in May 1498. The Zamorin of Calicut received him; the gifts, hats, washbasins and honey, fell below what his court expected from a king's envoy. The pepper and spices the fleet brought home in 1499 were worth many times the cost of the voyage.",
 why="Portugal wanted direct access to Asian spices, which reached Europe through Muslim and Venetian middlemen at high markups. Decades of Portuguese voyages down the African coast had built the ships and the navigational knowledge.",
 then="Portugal sent armed fleets from 1500, bombarded Calicut and imposed licensed trade, taking Goa in 1510, Malacca in 1511 and Hormuz in 1515. Indian Ocean commerce had been open to many merchant communities with no power controlling the sea; Portugal taxed and policed it by force, a model the Dutch and English companies expanded after 1600.",
 fact="The Zamorin of Calicut was unimpressed by da Gama's gifts: hats, washbasins and honey."),
"as15106": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese governor Afonso de Albuquerque wanted a permanent base on the Indian coast held by Portugal alone rather than rented from an Indian ruler. He took Goa from the Sultanate of Bijapur early in 1510, lost it and retook it that November with a larger force. Goa became the capital of Portugal's Asian empire for 451 years and set the pattern of fortified enclaves rather than conquest that European powers used in Asia for two centuries.",
 now="Goa gave Portugal the capital of an Asian empire and the Church a base for missions from Africa to Japan; it also set the pattern of fortified enclaves rather than conquest that European powers used in Asia for two centuries.",
 story="Portuguese governor Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Sultanate of Bijapur early in 1510, lost it to a Bijapur counterattack within months and retook it on 25 November 1510 with a larger force. He made it the capital of the Estado da Índia, Portugal's Asian empire.",
 why="Albuquerque wanted naval bases rather than large territories. Goa had a good harbour and controlled the trade in war horses from Persia and Arabia to the southern Indian kingdoms.",
 then="Goa became the seat of Portugal's viceroy, the base of Catholic missions from Africa to Japan, with an Inquisition from 1560, and a mixed Indo-Portuguese society. The Dutch and English bypassed it after 1600 and its trade declined.",
 fact="India took Goa by force in December 1961 after Portugal refused to leave; its Catholic culture still sets it apart."),
"as15269": dict(
 bluf="Timurid prince Babur, driven from his Central Asian homeland, wanted a kingdom and marched from Kabul on the Delhi Sultanate. At Panipat in April 1526 his field guns and mounted archers beat a far larger army and Delhi Sultan Ibrahim Lodi died on the field. The dynasty Babur founded ruled most of South Asia for two centuries and gave the subcontinent a Persian-influenced court culture, administration and architecture that outlasted it.",
 now="The Mughal tax and administrative system became the base on which the British built after 1765, and the standing of Muslim rulers in India's past is a live issue in Indian politics today.",
 story="Timurid prince Babur, descended from Timur and Genghis Khan, lost Samarkand and Fergana to the Uzbeks and ruled from Kabul from 1504. At Panipat on 21 April 1526 his field guns and mounted archers defeated the far larger army of Delhi Sultan Ibrahim Lodi, who died on the field. A year later at Khanwa he beat a Rajput confederacy under Rana Sanga of Mewar.",
 why="Babur needed a new kingdom after losing Central Asia. The Delhi Sultanate was split by feuds among its Afghan nobles, some of whom invited him in.",
 then="His son Humayun lost the throne to Sher Shah Suri in 1540 and regained it in 1555; his grandson Akbar, from 1556, built the lasting empire. Mughal rule integrated North India's administration, tax system and court culture.",
 fact="Babur wrote a frank memoir, the Baburnama, in which he complains that India has no good melons."),
"as155611": dict(
 bluf="Mughal Emperor Akbar set out to turn a conquest by a small Muslim ruling group into a durable empire over a mostly Hindu population. He brought Rajput rulers into the army and the imperial family, abolished the tax on non-Muslims and built a centralised land-revenue system. The accommodation kept the Mughals dominant in South Asia for 150 years, and its partial reversal under Aurangzeb after 1679 coincided with the revolts that weakened the empire.",
 now="Akbar's settlement with the Rajputs and his revenue system set the pattern of Mughal rule to 1707, and his pluralism is the precedent Indian secularists cite against Hindu nationalists in the debates of today.",
 story="Mughal Emperor Akbar came to the throne in 1556 aged 13 under his regent Bairam Khan and took personal control in 1560. He expanded the empire across North India, Gujarat and Bengal, married a Rajput princess of Amber in 1562, abolished the jizya tax on non-Muslims in 1564 and hosted debates between Muslim, Hindu, Christian, Jain and Zoroastrian theologians at Fatehpur Sikri.",
 why="The Mughals were a small Muslim ruling group over a mostly Hindu population. Lasting rule required local allies and a reliable tax base.",
 then="Rajput rulers became his generals and in-laws. A ranked imperial nobility, the mansabdars, and a land survey under finance minister Todar Mal fixed revenue. Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb reimposed the jizya in 1679 and alienated Rajput and Maratha allies; revolts followed.",
 fact="Akbar is thought to have been unable to read, yet built one of the greatest libraries of his age and had books read aloud to him."),
"sa-talikota": dict(
 bluf="Four Deccan sultanates allied to destroy Vijayanagara, the Hindu empire of South India whose regent had played them against one another for 20 years. At Talikota in January 1565 they beat its army, killed regent Rama Raya and sacked the capital at Hampi for months. The largest state in the south was gone, the Deccan fragmented, and the sultanates that won fell to the Mughals a century later.",
 now="The destruction of Vijayanagara removed the last large Hindu state in the south and opened the Deccan to the Mughals a century later; the sultanates that won fell to Aurangzeb in turn.",
 story="Vijayanagara's regent Rama Raya, ruling in the name of King Sadashiva Raya, had intervened in the sultanates' wars for two decades. The sultans of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar united against him and met his army near Talikota on 23 January 1565. Rama Raya was captured and beheaded. The victors sacked Hampi, then one of the largest cities in the world, for months.",
 why="The sultanates and Vijayanagara competed for the Raichur Doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers. Rama Raya's habit of playing the sultanates off against one another united them against him.",
 then="A smaller Vijayanagara state survived at Penukonda and later Chandragiri while its governors, the nayakas of Madurai, Tanjore and Gingee, became independent. The sultanates fought each other and were conquered by Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb, Bijapur in 1686 and Golconda in 1687.",
 fact="Hampi's ruins are a World Heritage site. Vijayanagara's armies included Muslim cavalry and the city had mosques, which its modern use as a symbol of Hindu resistance leaves out."),
"d-akbar-ibadat": dict(
 bluf="Mughal Emperor Akbar built a House of Worship to hear the faiths of his empire argued in front of him and to set the throne above the clerics. Debates among Muslim jurists widened within a few years to Hindus, Jains, Zoroastrians and Jesuits. Religious policy became the emperor's to decide, and his 'peace with all' underpinned Mughal rule over a Hindu-majority population until his successors moved back toward orthodoxy.",
 now="The policy of sulh-i kul set Mughal religious practice from 1579 until Aurangzeb's reign, and Akbar's court is the precedent cited in South Asia's debates over secularism.",
 story="In 1575 Mughal Emperor Akbar built the Ibadat Khana at his new capital, Fatehpur Sikri. Debates began among Muslim theologians, whose quarrels disappointed him; within a few years Hindu pandits, Jain monks, Zoroastrian priests and Jesuit priests from Goa took part. Akbar presided in person.",
 why="Akbar ruled a mostly non-Muslim empire, wanted to curb the influence of orthodox clerics over the throne and was curious about faith.",
 then="In 1579 a declaration drafted by his courtiers gave him the authority to decide disputed religious questions, and sulh-i kul, 'peace with all', guided appointments and law. Orthodox clerics resented it. Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb reimposed the jizya tax on non-Muslims in 1679.",
 fact="Akbar later devised the Din-i Ilahi, a court cult of his own, which drew only a handful of followers."),
"d-swally": dict(
 bluf="The English East India Company wanted a trading post at Surat, the Mughal Empire's main western port, and Portugal wanted to keep it out. Captain Thomas Best's two ships fought off a larger Portuguese squadron off Swally in November 1612. The Mughals licensed an English factory at Surat, and the Company's 335 years in India began with a trading permit rather than a conquest.",
 now="The Company's presence in India began as a licensed guest of the Mughal emperor, and it traded under Indian rulers' permission for 140 years before it took territory at Plassey in 1757.",
 story="In November 1612 two East India Company ships, the Red Dragon and the Hosiander, under Captain Thomas Best fought a Portuguese squadron of four galleons off Swally Hole near Surat over several days. Mughal officials watching from shore let the English open a trading post, a 'factory', at Surat. In 1615 Sir Thomas Roe arrived as English King James I's ambassador to Mughal Emperor Jahangir.",
 why="Portugal had controlled India's sea trade with Europe for a century and tried to keep rivals out. Mughal officials wanted a counterweight to Portuguese naval power, which preyed on their pilgrim ships.",
 then="Surat became the Company's main base in India until Bombay replaced it in 1687. Roe's embassy of 1615-19 secured trading privileges from the emperor.",
 fact="Best's flagship, the Red Dragon, is reported to have staged the first performance of 'Hamlet' outside Europe, off Sierra Leone in 1607."),
"as163217": dict(
 bluf="Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan set out to build a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal that would display his grief and the empire's wealth. About 20,000 workers finished the white marble complex over some 20 years. The Taj Mahal shows the Mughal state at its richest, and the taxation that paid for building on this scale and for Aurangzeb's wars strained the treasury the empire depended on.",
 now="The Taj records the Mughal state at its richest, when it could spend a province's revenue on a tomb; the treasury strain of such building and of Aurangzeb's wars is part of why the empire broke within a century.",
 story="Mumtaz Mahal died in childbirth at Burhanpur in June 1631. Mughal Emperor Shah Jahan began a white marble tomb for her at Agra on the Yamuna in 1632, with Ustad Ahmad Lahauri as chief architect. About 20,000 workers built it over some 20 years, inlaying the marble with semi-precious stones.",
 why="Shah Jahan's grief for his wife came first. The Mughal court used building to display wealth and piety.",
 then="Shah Jahan's building and wars strained the treasury. His son Aurangzeb seized power in 1658 after a war of succession and confined him in Agra Fort, where he died in 1666.",
 fact="Shah Jahan spent his last eight years a prisoner in Agra Fort, from which the tomb is visible across the river."),
"d-madras": dict(
 bluf="The East India Company wanted a fortified base of its own on the Coromandel coast for the cotton cloth trade, held on terms it set rather than as a guest. Company agent Francis Day obtained a land grant from a local Nayak ruler in 1639 and the Company built Fort St George. Madras became the first of the Company's three presidencies, and the shift from trading under Indian rulers to holding territory began there.",
 now="Fort St George was the model for the Company's fortified presidencies at Bombay and Calcutta, the bases from which it took territory in the 1700s.",
 story="In 1639 Company agent Francis Day obtained a grant of coastal land from a local Nayak ruler. The Company, with Andrew Cogan as its agent, built Fort St George, finished in 1644. Weavers, dyers and merchants settled around it, and the town of Madras grew up.",
 why="The Company wanted cheap Coromandel cotton cloth for the Asian and European markets and a fortified base it controlled.",
 then="Madras became the first of the Company's three presidencies, joined by Bombay in 1668 and Calcutta in 1690.",
 fact="Fort St George still houses the Tamil Nadu state legislature; Chennai, as Madras is now called, is one of India's largest cities."),
"sa-shivaji": dict(
 bluf="Maratha leader Shivaji Bhonsle set out to build an independent kingdom in the Western Ghats out of lands the Deccan sultanates and the Mughals claimed. He took hill forts, beat sultanate armies, escaped Mughal captivity and was crowned Chhatrapati at Raigad in June 1674. The Maratha state outlived him, tied down Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb for 25 years and became India's strongest power in the 1700s.",
 now="The Maratha state Shivaji founded fought the Mughals for the next 40 years and dominated India in the 1700s, and Shivaji is the founding hero of Marathi regional and Hindu nationalist politics today.",
 story="Maratha leader Shivaji Bhonsle, son of a general in Bijapur's service, took his first fort in 1646 and built a state in the Western Ghats through hill forts and fast-moving cavalry. He killed Bijapur general Afzal Khan at a parley in 1659, raided the Mughal port of Surat in 1664 and escaped Mughal custody at Agra in 1666. On 6 June 1674 he was crowned Chhatrapati, sovereign king, at Raigad.",
 why="Maratha warrior families wanted an independent state in place of service to the Deccan sultanates and the Mughals. The hills favoured guerrilla war over the sultanates' heavy armies.",
 then="Shivaji died in 1680. Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb spent his last 25 years fighting the Marathas in the Deccan. After his death the Marathas expanded across much of India, until the Afghans beat them at Panipat in 1761 and the British in 1818.",
 fact="Mumbai's main airport and its main railway station bear Shivaji's name."),
"d-calcutta": dict(
 bluf="The East India Company wanted a secure port in Bengal, the richest province of Mughal India, after losing a short war with the Mughal governor. Company agent Job Charnock settled at Sutanuti on the Hooghly in August 1690, and the Company bought three villages and built Fort William. Calcutta became the base from which the Company took Bengal in 1757 and the capital of British India until 1911.",
 now="Calcutta was the Company's base for the conquest of Bengal in 1757 and the seat of British rule in India until 1911; Bengal's revenue paid for the conquest of the rest.",
 story="The Company had fought a short war with the Mughal governor of Bengal from 1686 and lost. Company agent Job Charnock returned under a Mughal pardon and settled at Sutanuti on the Hooghly on 24 August 1690. In 1698 the Company bought the rights to the villages of Sutanuti, Kalikata and Gobindapur, and Fort William, begun in 1696, was built beside them.",
 why="Bengal produced silk, cotton cloth and saltpetre for gunpowder, and the Company needed a port it could fortify.",
 then="Calcutta became the base from which Company commander Robert Clive won Bengal at Plassey in 1757. It was the capital of British India until 1911 and grew into the second city of the empire.",
 fact="The Calcutta High Court ruled in 2003 that the city had no founder, since the site was settled before Charnock arrived."),
"as170722": dict(
 bluf="Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb spent his reign extending the empire to its greatest size and holding it by force against Marathas, Rajputs and Sikhs. He died in 1707 aged 88 with the Deccan unconquered and the treasury strained by 25 years of war. His sons fought for the throne, and within 30 years the provinces were states in all but name, which the East India Company exploited from 1757.",
 now="The succession wars after 1707 turned Bengal, Awadh and Hyderabad into hereditary states by the 1720s, the fragmentation the East India Company picked apart from 1757. Aurangzeb's religious policy is a live quarrel in Indian politics today.",
 story="Mughal Emperor Aurangzeb pushed the empire to its greatest size, taking Bijapur in 1686 and Golconda in 1687, and spent his last 25 years campaigning against the Marathas in the Deccan. He reimposed the jizya tax on non-Muslims in 1679 and executed the Sikh Guru Tegh Bahadur in 1675. He died in the Deccan on 3 March 1707 aged 88. Three of his sons fought over the throne; Bahadur Shah I won.",
 why="Decades of war in the Deccan had drained the treasury and the nobility's patience. His religious policy had cost him Rajput and other Hindu allies.",
 then="Six emperors followed in twelve years. The governors of Bengal, Awadh and Hyderabad made their posts hereditary in the 1720s, and the Marathas expanded north. The East India Company exploited the divisions from 1757.",
 fact="Aurangzeb asked to be buried in a plain open grave at Khuldabad, paid for with money he had earned sewing caps."),
"as173923": dict(
 bluf="Persian ruler Nader Shah marched on Delhi to seize the wealth of a Mughal empire whose border defences had collapsed. He beat the Mughal army at Karnal in February 1739, occupied Delhi and left with the Peacock Throne, the Koh-i-Noor and treasure worth years of Mughal revenue. The emperor's weakness was now plain to every regional ruler and European company in India.",
 now="The sack confirmed that the Mughal emperor could no longer defend his capital; regional rulers, the Marathas and the European companies acted on that from the 1740s.",
 story="Persian ruler Nader Shah crossed the Indus and crushed the army of Mughal Emperor Muhammad Shah at Karnal on 24 February 1739. He entered Delhi; after a rumour of his death set off attacks that killed some of his soldiers, he ordered a massacre on 22 March in which perhaps 20,000 or more people died. He left in May with the Peacock Throne and the Koh-i-Noor diamond.",
 why="Nader Shah was drawn by Mughal wealth and a weak emperor. Mughal border defences in Kabul and the Punjab had collapsed.",
 then="Mughal weakness was laid bare. Regional rulers, the Marathas and the British and French companies drew their own conclusions. Afghan ruler Ahmad Shah Durrani, once Nader Shah's officer, raided India repeatedly from 1748.",
 fact="The haul was so large that Nader Shah suspended taxes in Persia for three years. The Koh-i-Noor is now in the British Crown Jewels, and India still asks for it back."),
"as175724": dict(
 bluf="East India Company commander Robert Clive marched against Bengal Nawab Siraj ud-Daulah to recover Calcutta and secure the Company's trade. He won at Plassey on 23 June 1757 after bribing the Nawab's commander Mir Jafar to hold his troops back. A trading company became the power behind the throne in India's richest province, and Bengal's revenue paid for the conquest of the rest.",
 now="Plassey gave the Company Bengal's land revenue from 1765, which funded its armies and the conquest of India over the next 60 years. Indian nationalists date the 'drain' of India's wealth to Britain from it.",
 story="Bengal Nawab Siraj ud-Daulah captured Calcutta in June 1756. Company commander Robert Clive retook it in January 1757 and marched north. Before the battle Clive secretly promised the throne to the Nawab's commander Mir Jafar, who held his troops back. On 23 June 1757 Clive's army of about 3,000 beat the Nawab's of about 50,000 at Plassey.",
 why="The Nawab resented the Company's fortifications and its abuse of trade privileges. Clive plotted with discontented Bengali nobles and the Jagat Seth bankers.",
 trig="The Nawab's capture of Calcutta in June 1756 and the deaths of British prisoners in the 'Black Hole', which the Company used to justify war.",
 then="The Company installed Mir Jafar as Nawab and extracted payments running to millions of pounds. After Buxar in 1764 it won the right to collect Bengal's land revenue in 1765. Bengal's taxes paid for further conquest, and Company misrule contributed to the Bengal famine of 1770, which killed millions.",
 fact="Clive's about 3,000 troops beat about 50,000 because Mir Jafar stood aside; the Company counted around 20 dead."),
"sa-panipat": dict(
 bluf="Afghan ruler Ahmad Shah Durrani marched into India to end the Maratha advance into the Punjab; the Marathas fought to hold the north of the decaying Mughal empire. On 14 January 1761 his army destroyed the Maratha force, killing tens of thousands of soldiers and camp followers. Maratha power in the north was broken for a decade and the Afghans went home, leaving a vacuum the East India Company, which had taken Bengal in 1757, moved into.",
 now="Panipat removed the one Indian power able to unite the north just as the Company took Bengal; for the next 40 years the Company faced divided opponents and beat them one at a time.",
 story="The Marathas had taken Lahore in 1758, challenging Afghan ruler Ahmad Shah Durrani, founder of the Afghan state. The Maratha army under Sadashivrao Bhau camped at Panipat north of Delhi through late 1760, cut off from supplies. On 14 January 1761 Durrani's army, with allies from Awadh and the Rohilla Afghans, destroyed it; tens of thousands of soldiers and camp followers were killed or massacred.",
 why="Rivalry for control of the decaying Mughal Empire's northern lands came first. The Marathas had alienated the Rajputs, Jats and Sikhs, who gave them no help.",
 then="Maratha power in the north was broken for a decade, and Peshwa Balaji Baji Rao died within months. The Afghans withdrew, and Sikh chiefs filled the Punjab. The East India Company won Buxar in 1764 and Bengal's revenue in 1765.",
 fact="A Maratha letter reported the loss in a jeweller's terms: two pearls dissolved, 27 gold coins lost, and the silver and copper beyond counting."),
"sa-tipu": dict(
 bluf="The East India Company set out to destroy Mysore ruler Tipu Sultan, the strongest Indian power in the south, before his approach to Napoleon's France could bring French help. Company troops stormed Seringapatam on 4 May 1799 and Tipu died defending the breach. Southern India was secure for the Company, and the war showed that an Indian state that had copied European arms still could not match the Company's resources.",
 now="Mysore's defeat left the Marathas as the Company's only rival in India, beaten in turn by 1818. Tipu's standing is contested in Indian politics: an anti-colonial hero to one side, a persecutor of Hindus and Christians to the other.",
 story="Mysore under Hyder Ali and his son Tipu Sultan fought the Company in four wars from 1767. Tipu, 'the Tiger of Mysore', used iron-cased war rockets, reformed his army on European lines and sent envoys to France. After the third war of 1790-92 he lost half his territory. In May 1799 Company troops under General George Harris stormed Seringapatam and Tipu died in the fighting.",
 why="Governor-General Richard Wellesley feared Tipu's correspondence with France, whose army under Napoleon was in Egypt in 1798, and wanted to destroy the Company's strongest rival in the south.",
 then="The Company restored the Wodeyar dynasty as dependent rulers of a smaller Mysore and annexed the coast. It turned on the Marathas in 1803.",
 fact="Mysore's iron-cased rockets inspired Britain's Congreve rockets, the 'rockets' red glare' of the American national anthem."),
"sa-maratha1818": dict(
 bluf="Governor-General the Marquess of Hastings set out to end the Maratha confederacy as an independent power and to stop Pindari raiding from Maratha lands. Company armies beat Peshwa Baji Rao II and the other Maratha chiefs in the Third Anglo-Maratha War of 1817-18. The Company was now the paramount power in India, ruling directly or through dependent princes, a system that lasted until 1947.",
 now="The paramountcy established in 1818 made the Company and then the Crown the arbiter of every Indian state until 1947, when more than 500 princely states had to be absorbed into India and Pakistan.",
 story="Peshwa Baji Rao II, nominal head of the Maratha confederacy, attacked the Company residency at Pune in November 1817. Company armies under Governor-General the Marquess of Hastings beat the Peshwa, Holkar of Indore and the Bhonsle of Nagpur in a campaign through the winter of 1817-18. The Peshwa surrendered in June 1818 and was pensioned off at Bithur near Kanpur.",
 why="Hastings set out to eliminate rival Indian powers and end raiding by Pindari bands based in Maratha territory. The Maratha chiefs had failed to act together since 1803.",
 then="The Peshwa's office was abolished and his lands annexed to the Bombay Presidency. Much of India was now ruled through princely states bound by treaty to the Company. Nana Sahib, the last Peshwa's adopted heir, was refused his pension and became a leader of the 1857 rebellion.",
 fact="The Peshwa's pension of 800,000 rupees a year was among the largest the Company ever paid."),
"as185732": dict(
 bluf="Sepoys of the Bengal army mutinied at Meerut over cartridges rumoured to be greased with cow and pig fat, and princes, landlords and peasants across northern India rose with them to end Company rule. British forces retook Delhi and Lucknow by 1858, with atrocities on both sides. Company rule and the Mughal dynasty ended, and the Crown governed India afterward through princes and landlords, with Hindus and Muslims counted as separate blocs.",
 now="The rebellion ended Company rule and the policy of reform; the Crown governed India after 1858 through the princes and landlords, kept Indians out of the army's command and treated Hindus and Muslims as separate blocs, a structure that shaped partition ninety years later.",
 story="On 10 May 1857 Indian soldiers, sepoys, of the Bengal army at Meerut mutinied after 85 comrades were jailed for refusing cartridges rumoured to be greased with cow and pig fat, offensive to Hindus and Muslims alike. They marched to Delhi and proclaimed the aged Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah II their leader. Revolt spread across Awadh and central India; rebels killed British civilians at Kanpur, and British forces killed captured and suspected rebels in large numbers. British forces retook Delhi in September 1857 and Lucknow in March 1858.",
 why="Annexations such as Awadh in 1856 and the lost status of landed elites came first. Heavy land taxes, fear of forced Christianisation and sepoy grievances over pay and service overseas added fuel.",
 trig="The cartridge controversy and the jailing of 85 sepoys at Meerut on 9 May 1857 for refusing to use them.",
 then="Britain abolished the Company and ruled India directly through a viceroy from 1858; Bahadur Shah II was exiled to Rangoon. Britain stopped annexing princely states, recruited soldiers from groups it considered loyal, Sikhs, Gurkhas and Punjabi Muslims among them, and kept British society more segregated from Indian.",
 fact="Indian nationalists call it the First War of Independence; British writers called it the Mutiny."),
"as185833": dict(
 bluf="Parliament took India from the East India Company to govern it directly after the rebellion of 1857. A viceroy and a civil service ruled from Calcutta, building railways, courts and an English-educated elite while extracting revenue and presiding over famines. The Raj lasted 89 years, and the elite it created founded the Congress that ended it.",
 now="India's railways, legal system, civil service and use of English are Raj legacies, and how to weigh them against its famines and extraction is fiercely debated in India and Britain.",
 story="The Government of India Act of 1858 transferred power from the East India Company to the British Crown. Queen Victoria's proclamation of 1 November 1858 promised religious tolerance and equal treatment under law. A viceroy ruled from Calcutta, from 1911 New Delhi, through a civil service of around 1,000 British officers over a population of some 200 million.",
 why="The 1857 rebellion convinced Parliament that a trading company could not govern India.",
 then="British rule lasted 89 years. Famines in 1876-78 and 1896-1900 killed millions. The English-educated middle class founded the Indian National Congress in 1885, which led the independence movement.",
 fact="The Indian Civil Service never had much more than 1,000 officers; by 1900 they administered nearly 300 million people."),
"as187737": dict(
 bluf="British Prime Minister Benjamin Disraeli wanted to bind India's princes to the Crown and give Queen Victoria a title to match the emperors of Germany and Russia. The Royal Titles Act of 1876 made her Empress of India, proclaimed at a Delhi Durbar on 1 January 1877 while famine spread across southern India. The Raj presented itself as an imperial court of princes for the next 70 years, and the gap between its pageantry and the famine dead fed Indian criticism of British rule.",
 now="The title bound the princes to the Crown as feudatories, the structure through which Britain ruled about a third of India's territory until 1947.",
 story="Under the Royal Titles Act of 1876, British Prime Minister Benjamin Disraeli made Queen Victoria 'Empress of India'. Viceroy Lord Lytton proclaimed the title at a Delhi Durbar on 1 January 1877 attended by the Indian princes, while famine spread across the Deccan and the Madras Presidency.",
 why="Disraeli wanted to tie India's princes to the Crown and match the prestige of the German and Russian emperors. Victoria wanted the title.",
 then="The famine of 1876-78 killed millions while the celebrations went on; estimates range from about 5 million to 10 million. Indian critics such as Dadabhai Naoroji argued that British rule was draining India's wealth. Durbars followed in 1903 and 1911.",
 fact="Victoria never visited India; she learned Urdu from her Indian attendant Abdul Karim."),
"as188538": dict(
 bluf="Western-educated Indians wanted a national body to ask for a larger Indian role in government, and retired British official Allan Octavian Hume helped them found one. Seventy-two delegates met in Bombay in December 1885 and petitioned for reform within British rule. Under Mohandas Gandhi the Congress became the mass movement that won independence in 1947, and it governed India for most of its first 50 years.",
 now="It ruled India for most of its first 50 years, and its decline since the 1990s has reshaped Indian politics around the Hindu-nationalist BJP.",
 story="In December 1885, 72 delegates, mostly lawyers, teachers and journalists, met in Bombay to found the Indian National Congress, with retired civil servant Allan Octavian Hume as organiser and Calcutta barrister W. C. Bonnerjee as president. The early resolutions asked for more Indians in the civil service and the legislative councils.",
 why="Western-educated Indians wanted a voice in how India was governed. The European backlash of 1883 against the Ilbert Bill, which would have let Indian judges try Europeans, showed them a discrimination they could organise against.",
 then="Congress split between moderates and extremists in 1907 and reunited in 1916. Under Mohandas Gandhi from 1920 and Jawaharlal Nehru it became a mass movement and led India to independence in 1947.",
 fact="Hume, the British founder, was an ornithologist whose collection of Indian birds went to the Natural History Museum in London."),
"sa-bengal": dict(
 bluf="Viceroy Lord Curzon divided Bengal, a province of some 78 million people, into a Hindu-majority west and a Muslim-majority east, for administrative efficiency in his account and to weaken Bengali politics in his critics' account. Bengali Hindus answered with the Swadeshi boycott of British goods, India's first mass nationalist campaign. Britain reversed the partition in 1911, which showed that protest could change policy, and the Hindu-Muslim line it drew reappeared in 1947.",
 now="The partition and its reversal set both communities' politics: Hindu Bengalis learned that mass protest worked, Muslim leaders learned that Congress would resist a Muslim province, and the 1905 line roughly anticipated the border of East Pakistan in 1947.",
 story="Viceroy Lord Curzon divided Bengal on 16 October 1905, creating a province of Eastern Bengal and Assam with its capital at Dhaka and a Muslim majority. Bengali Hindus saw divide-and-rule and launched the Swadeshi movement, boycotting British cloth and burning it in public, founding national schools and Indian-owned mills. Some turned to bombs; Bengal's revolutionary societies date from these years.",
 why="Officially administrative efficiency in a province of 78 million. Nationalists read an attempt to split Bengal's Hindu political class from its base.",
 then="Britain reversed the partition at the Delhi Durbar of December 1911 and moved the capital to Delhi. Separate Muslim electorates, granted in 1909, kept the communal divide in the political system. Muslim leaders, who had gained a province and lost it, founded the Muslim League at Dhaka in 1906.",
 fact="Rabindranath Tagore wrote 'Amar Shonar Bangla' for the anti-partition movement in 1905; it is now Bangladesh's national anthem."),
"sa-muslimleague": dict(
 bluf="Muslim notables led by Aga Khan III and Nawab Salimullah of Dhaka wanted a body to defend Muslim interests against a Hindu-majority Congress and to secure a Muslim share of power. They founded the All-India Muslim League at Dhaka in December 1906 and won separate Muslim electorates in 1909. Under Muhammad Ali Jinnah the League demanded a Muslim state in 1940 and won Pakistan in 1947.",
 now="Its argument that India's Muslims formed a separate nation led to Pakistan's creation in 1947, and the 'two-nation' idea still divides India and Pakistan.",
 story="On 30 December 1906 Muslim notables meeting at Dhaka, among them Aga Khan III and Nawab Salimullah of Dhaka, founded the All-India Muslim League. The Morley-Minto reforms of 1909 gave Muslims separate electorates. Muhammad Ali Jinnah, a Congress member who joined the League in 1913, led it from the 1930s, and at Lahore in March 1940 it resolved that Muslim-majority areas should form independent states.",
 why="Muslim elites feared domination by the Hindu-majority Congress. The Hindu campaign against the 1905 partition of Bengal, which had given them a Muslim-majority province, alarmed them. A deputation to Viceroy Lord Minto at Simla in October 1906 had already won a promise of separate representation.",
 then="Congress accepted separate electorates in the Lucknow Pact of 1916. The League won most Muslim seats in the 1946 elections and Pakistan in August 1947.",
 fact="Jinnah, who led the League to Pakistan, had been called the 'ambassador of Hindu-Muslim unity' by Congress leader Sarojini Naidu in the 1910s."),
"sa-amritsar": dict(
 bluf="Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer set out to make an example of a crowd that had defied his ban on gatherings during unrest over the Rowlatt Acts. His troops fired without warning on thousands in Jallianwala Bagh on 13 April 1919, killing 379 by the official count and around 1,000 by Indian estimates. Moderate Indians who had trusted British promises turned to demanding independence, and Gandhi's mass campaign of 1920 had its cause.",
 now="The massacre moved Congress from petitioning to mass non-cooperation within a year. Britain has expressed regret, in 1997, 2013 and 2019, and declined a formal apology, which keeps Amritsar central to the argument over how Britain remembers its empire.",
 story="Unrest over the Rowlatt Acts and the arrest of two local leaders had led to riots in Amritsar on 10 April 1919 in which five Europeans were killed. Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer banned gatherings. On 13 April, the Baisakhi festival, thousands gathered in Jallianwala Bagh, a walled garden with narrow exits. Dyer marched in about 50 riflemen and ordered them to fire without warning; they fired about 1,650 rounds in ten minutes. The official count was 379 dead; Indian estimates ran to around 1,000.",
 why="Unrest over the Rowlatt Acts, which extended wartime detention without trial, came first. Dyer meant to terrorise the Punjab into order, and Punjab Governor Michael O'Dwyer backed him.",
 then="The Hunter Committee censured Dyer in 1920 and he was retired; the House of Lords voted in his favour and a public subscription raised £26,000 for him. Rabindranath Tagore renounced his knighthood. Mohandas Gandhi launched the Non-Cooperation Movement in 1920, and moderates who had trusted British promises turned to demanding independence.",
 fact="Udham Singh, who had been in Amritsar in 1919, shot Michael O'Dwyer dead in London in 1940."),
"as193052": dict(
 bluf="Mohandas Gandhi chose the salt tax, which every Indian paid, as the issue on which to launch civil disobedience against British rule. He walked 240 miles to the sea at Dandi and broke the salt law on 6 April 1930; millions followed with illegal salt, cloth boycotts and tax refusal, and more than 60,000 were jailed. Indian independence became a mass movement, and Britain negotiated with Gandhi as an equal in 1931.",
 now="The march made non-violent mass disobedience the method of the Indian movement to 1947 and the template Martin Luther King Jr. and later movements adopted.",
 story="On 12 March 1930 Mohandas Gandhi set out from Sabarmati ashram near Ahmedabad with 78 followers and walked about 385 km (240 miles) to the sea at Dandi. On 6 April he picked up a lump of natural salt, breaking the law that made salt a government monopoly. Millions followed by making or buying illegal salt, boycotting British cloth and refusing taxes. At the Dharasana salt works in May police beat unresisting marchers, and American journalist Webb Miller's report reached newspapers worldwide.",
 why="Gandhi chose the salt tax as a simple injustice that every Indian understood. Congress had resolved on full independence at Lahore in December 1929 and needed a campaign.",
 then="More than 60,000 people were arrested, Gandhi among them in May. Viceroy Lord Irwin negotiated the Gandhi-Irwin Pact of March 1931, and Gandhi attended the Round Table Conference in London that year as Congress's sole delegate.",
 fact="Time magazine named Gandhi its Man of the Year for 1930."),
"sa-quitindia": dict(
 bluf="Mohandas Gandhi and the Congress demanded that Britain leave India at once, with Japan on the border, to force a settlement before the war ended. Britain jailed the leadership within hours of the resolution of 8 August 1942 and crushed the protests. Congress sat in prison until 1945 while the Muslim League organised, which made partition more likely when Britain did leave five years later.",
 now="The movement showed that Britain could hold India only by force, which it would not have after the war; independence and partition followed within five years.",
 story="After the Cripps mission of March 1942 offered dominion status after the war and Congress rejected it, Congress met in Bombay and passed the Quit India resolution on 8 August 1942, with Mohandas Gandhi's call to 'do or die'. Within hours Gandhi, Jawaharlal Nehru and the leadership were arrested. Strikes, sabotage of railways and attacks on police stations followed; the government used troops and arrested tens of thousands.",
 why="Britain had declared India at war in 1939 without consulting Indians. The Cripps offer gave too little, and Japan's advance to Burma made Gandhi want Britain out before an invasion.",
 then="Congress leaders stayed in prison until 1945. The Muslim League, which opposed the movement, grew in membership and won most Muslim seats in the 1946 elections, strengthening its demand for Pakistan.",
 fact="Gandhi's wife Kasturba died in detention at the Aga Khan Palace in Pune in 1944."),
"as194757": dict(
 bluf="Britain, exhausted by the war, set out to leave India fast; Congress wanted a united India and the Muslim League wanted Pakistan, and they could not agree. Viceroy Lord Mountbatten brought the handover forward to August 1947, and British India became Hindu-majority India and Muslim-majority Pakistan. About 15 million people crossed the new borders, estimates of the dead run from about 200,000 to two million, and the two states fought over Kashmir within months and have faced each other with nuclear weapons since 1998.",
 now="India and Pakistan are nuclear-armed rivals, and the memory of Partition feeds nationalist narratives and communal tensions in both countries.",
 story="Britain decided after the war to leave India quickly. Congress under Jawaharlal Nehru and the Muslim League under Muhammad Ali Jinnah could not agree on a united India, and the Cabinet Mission plan of 1946 collapsed. The last viceroy, Lord Mountbatten, brought the handover forward from June 1948 to August 1947. British lawyer Cyril Radcliffe drew the borders through Punjab and Bengal in five weeks; they were published two days after independence. About 15 million people crossed the new borders. Estimates of the dead range from about 200,000, in British official estimates of the time, to two million, in later Indian and Pakistani accounts.",
 why="Decades of Hindu-Muslim political division, hardened by separate electorates since 1909, came first. The Muslim League's demand for Pakistan from 1940 and Congress's refusal of a weak federation followed. Britain's weakness and haste, and the Calcutta killings of August 1946 that showed what a contested settlement would cost, narrowed the options.",
 trig="Britain's announcement in February 1947 that it would leave by June 1948, and Mountbatten's advance of the date to August 1947.",
 then="India and Pakistan went to war over Kashmir within months. Refugees reshaped Delhi, Lahore and Karachi. Pakistan itself split in 1971 when Bangladesh won independence.",
 fact="The border was drawn in about five weeks by a British lawyer, Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India."),
"sa-kashmir": dict(
 bluf="India and Pakistan each set out to secure Kashmir, a Muslim-majority princely state whose Hindu ruler had chosen neither. Pakistani tribal fighters invaded in October 1947, Maharaja Hari Singh acceded to India and Indian troops held Srinagar; a UN ceasefire on 1 January 1949 left the state divided. The ceasefire line became the Line of Control, and Kashmir became the core of a rivalry that has produced two more wars and repeated crises between nuclear-armed states.",
 now="Kashmir has been the central dispute between India and Pakistan since 1947 and the cause of wars in 1965 and 1999, between states that have held nuclear weapons since 1998.",
 story="Kashmir's Hindu ruler Maharaja Hari Singh had signed a standstill agreement with Pakistan and delayed choosing. In October 1947 tribal fighters from Pakistan's North-West Frontier invaded and advanced on Srinagar; Pakistan says they acted on their own, India says Pakistan organised them. Hari Singh signed an Instrument of Accession to India on 26 October, and Indian troops flew into Srinagar the next day. Pakistan's army joined the fighting in 1948. A UN ceasefire on 1 January 1949 left the state divided along what became the Line of Control.",
 why="The Muslim-majority state's Hindu ruler hesitated between India and Pakistan. India holds that the accession settled Kashmir's status; Pakistan holds that a Muslim-majority state's accession required the plebiscite the UN Security Council called for in 1948, which has never been held.",
 then="India and Pakistan fought over Kashmir again in 1965 and at Kargil in 1999. An insurgency in Indian-administered Kashmir began in 1989; India attributes it to Pakistani sponsorship, Pakistan calls it an indigenous rising. India revoked the state's constitutional autonomy in August 2019.",
 fact="The ceasefire line was left undefined north of map point NJ9842; since 1984 Indian and Pakistani troops have held posts on the Siachen glacier beyond it at over 5,000 metres."),
"sa-gandhi": dict(
 bluf="Hindu nationalist Nathuram Godse set out to kill Mohandas Gandhi for conciliating Muslims and Pakistan after Partition. He shot Gandhi at a prayer meeting in New Delhi on 30 January 1948. The killing discredited Hindu nationalism in Indian politics for decades, and Indian Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru's secular line went unchallenged at the top for a generation.",
 now="The assassination kept Hindu nationalist parties at the margin of Indian politics until the 1980s; the RSS, banned after the killing, is the parent body of the BJP that has governed India since 2014.",
 story="On 30 January 1948 Nathuram Godse, a Hindu nationalist from Pune and a former member of the RSS, shot Mohandas Gandhi three times at a prayer meeting at Birla House in New Delhi. Godse blamed Gandhi for Partition and for conciliating Muslims and Pakistan, including his fast that January which pressed India to pay Pakistan its share of assets. Godse and his associate Narayan Apte were hanged in November 1949.",
 why="Gandhi had fasted to stop communal violence in Delhi and to make India pay Pakistan its share of assets. Hindu nationalists held him responsible for the losses of Partition.",
 then="The government banned the RSS in February 1948 and lifted the ban in July 1949. Hindu nationalism stayed marginal in Indian politics until the 1980s. Godse's statement at his trial circulates among his admirers, and arguments over his standing recur in Indian politics.",
 fact="Gandhi had survived a bomb thrown at the same prayer ground ten days earlier, on 20 January 1948."),
"sa-constitution": dict(
 bluf="India's Constituent Assembly set out to give a poor, diverse and largely illiterate country a democratic republic with equal rights and a vote for every adult. The constitution drafted by a committee under Law Minister B. R. Ambedkar took effect on 26 January 1950. India became the world's largest democracy, and its courts, federalism and secular clauses have framed every political fight since.",
 now="India is the world's largest democracy, and arguments over its secular character and the reach of its courts are at the centre of its politics today.",
 story="The Constituent Assembly met from December 1946. A drafting committee chaired by Law Minister B. R. Ambedkar, a jurist from the 'untouchable' Dalit community, produced the text, adopted on 26 November 1949 and in force on 26 January 1950, the anniversary of Congress's independence pledge of 1930. It guaranteed equal rights, abolished untouchability, gave every adult the vote and reserved seats for Scheduled Castes and Tribes. With 395 articles it was the longest constitution in the world.",
 why="Independence required a new constitutional order for a vast, diverse country. The Congress leadership had committed itself to universal suffrage and a secular state since the 1930s.",
 then="The first general election, in 1951-52, had over 170 million eligible voters, the largest ever held at the time. Ambedkar resigned from the cabinet in 1951 and converted to Buddhism with hundreds of thousands of followers in 1956.",
 fact="The original constitution was handwritten in calligraphy by Prem Behari Narain Raizada and is kept in a helium-filled case in Parliament."),
"sa-sinoindian": dict(
 bluf="China set out to end Indian patrols pushing into territory it held in Aksai Chin and to settle the Himalayan border by force; India's leaders had bet that China would not attack. Chinese forces attacked on 20 October 1962, routed the Indian army in both sectors and declared a ceasefire a month later, keeping Aksai Chin. India's leadership was humiliated, the border stayed undefined, and the two largest countries in Asia have faced each other across it since.",
 now="The border has never been agreed; troops killed each other in the Galwan valley in June 2020, the first deaths there since 1975.",
 story="Disputes had run for years over Aksai Chin, where China had built a road to Tibet in the 1950s, and over the McMahon Line in the east, and India had sheltered the Dalai Lama since 1959. Chinese forces attacked on 20 October 1962 in Ladakh and across the McMahon Line. They routed Indian forces and reached the foothills of Assam, then declared a unilateral ceasefire on 21 November, withdrawing in the east and keeping Aksai Chin.",
 why="China had rejected the McMahon Line, drawn by Britain in 1914, and India had rejected China's hold on Aksai Chin, through which the road to Tibet ran. India's 'forward policy' of 1961 pushed patrols into contested areas. China's reading is of a defensive strike against encroachment; India's is of unprovoked aggression.",
 then="Indian Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru's standing never recovered, and he died in 1964. India enlarged its army and turned to the Soviet Union for arms. China tested a nuclear bomb in October 1964, and India followed in 1974.",
 fact="The war fell in the same weeks as the Cuban missile crisis, and the United States airlifted arms to India while Washington and Moscow faced off over Cuba."),
"sa-bangladesh": dict(
 bluf="Bengali nationalists under Sheikh Mujibur Rahman set out to govern Pakistan after winning its 1970 election and, when West Pakistan's rulers refused, to leave it. Pakistan's army launched a crackdown in March 1971 that drove about 10 million refugees into India and killed, by Bangladesh's count, 3 million people and by most outside estimates hundreds of thousands; India invaded in December and Pakistan surrendered in two weeks. Pakistan lost more than half its population, India became the dominant power in South Asia, and a state built on religion had split along language.",
 now="The war fixed India's primacy in South Asia and set off Pakistan's drive for a nuclear weapon from 1972. In Bangladesh the count of the dead and the trials of collaborators since 2010 remain the dividing line of its politics.",
 story="Sheikh Mujibur Rahman's Awami League won 160 of 300 seats in Pakistan's December 1970 election. Pakistani President Yahya Khan and West Pakistani leader Zulfikar Ali Bhutto postponed the assembly. On the night of 25 March 1971 the army launched Operation Searchlight in Dhaka and arrested Mujib. Guerrillas of the Mukti Bahini fought through the year with Indian support, and about 10 million refugees crossed into India. Deaths over the nine months are put at 3 million by the Bangladesh government, at 26,000 by Pakistan's Hamoodur Rahman Commission of 1974 and in the hundreds of thousands by most outside estimates. Indian forces invaded on 3 December after Pakistani air strikes on Indian airfields, and Pakistan's eastern command surrendered in Dhaka on 16 December with about 93,000 prisoners.",
 why="West Pakistan dominated politics, the army and the economy, and the attempt to make Urdu the national language had provoked Bengali resistance since the Language Movement of 1952. The two wings were 1,600 km apart with India between them.",
 trig="The army's crackdown, Operation Searchlight, from 25 March 1971, after Pakistan's leaders refused to let the election winners govern.",
 then="Bangladesh became independent and Pakistan lost more than half its population. The United States and China backed Pakistan while the Soviet Union backed India under a treaty signed in August 1971. Pakistani Prime Minister Zulfikar Ali Bhutto launched a nuclear weapons programme in 1972. Mujib was assassinated in a coup in August 1975.",
 fact="The surrender at Dhaka of about 93,000 men was the largest since the Second World War."),
"sa-pokhran1974": dict(
 bluf="Indian Prime Minister Indira Gandhi's government set out to show that India could build a nuclear device, ten years after China's first test. The device exploded under the Rajasthan desert at Pokhran on 18 May 1974, which India called a 'peaceful nuclear explosion'. India became the first state outside the five recognised nuclear powers to test, supplier states formed the Nuclear Suppliers Group to tighten exports, and Pakistan's own programme gained urgency.",
 now="India and Pakistan both openly tested weapons in 1998, and South Asia now has two nuclear-armed rivals that have fought a limited war and several crises since.",
 story="Code-named 'Smiling Buddha', the test of 18 May 1974 at Pokhran in Rajasthan used plutonium from the Canadian-supplied CIRUS reactor. The Indian Atomic Energy Commission under chairman Homi Sethna and physicist Raja Ramanna ran it. India called it a peaceful nuclear explosion; it made India the first nation outside the five permanent Security Council members to explode a nuclear device.",
 why="China's nuclear weapons, tested from 1964, two years after it defeated India, came first. Indian Prime Minister Indira Gandhi wanted a demonstration of standing after the victory of 1971.",
 then="Canada and the United States cut nuclear cooperation. Supplier states formed the Nuclear Suppliers Group in 1975 to control nuclear exports. Pakistan's programme, begun in 1972, accelerated. India tested again only in 1998.",
 fact="The test fell on Buddha's birthday, hence the codename; the message to Indira Gandhi read 'the Buddha has smiled'."),
"d-saur": dict(
 bluf="Officers loyal to the communist People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan set out to seize power before Afghan President Mohammed Daoud Khan could finish arresting the party's leaders. They stormed the palace on 27 April 1978 and killed Daoud and most of his family. The new government's forced reforms provoked a rebellion that drew in the Soviet Union, and Afghanistan has been at war almost without a break since.",
 now="It began more than four decades of war in Afghanistan, which ended with the Taliban back in power in 2021.",
 story="Afghan President Mohammed Daoud Khan had cracked down on the communist party after the killing of party ideologue Mir Akbar Khyber on 17 April 1978 and arrested its leaders. On 27 April army and air force officers loyal to the party stormed the palace in Kabul and killed Daoud and most of his family. Nur Muhammad Taraki became president with Hafizullah Amin as his deputy. The government decreed land reform, the cancellation of rural debts and equal rights for women, and arrested or executed thousands of opponents.",
 why="Daoud's crackdown on the party after Khyber's killing came first. The party had built cells in the armed forces since the 1960s.",
 then="Revolt spread to most provinces by 1979; a rising in Herat in March 1979 killed Soviet advisers. Amin had Taraki killed in the autumn of 1979. The Soviet Union invaded in December 1979 and killed Amin.",
 fact="Saur is the month of the Afghan solar calendar in which the coup fell."),
"as197968b": dict(
 bluf="The Soviet leadership sent troops into Afghanistan to replace an ally it distrusted and to hold a communist government against rebellion. Soviet forces killed Afghan leader Hafizullah Amin, installed Babrak Karmal and fought the mujahideen for nine years, losing about 15,000 soldiers while perhaps a million or more Afghans died. The war drained the Soviet Union in its last decade, and the resistance networks the United States, Saudi Arabia and Pakistan armed produced the jihadist movements of the 1990s.",
 now="Afghanistan has seen almost continuous war since 1979, and the Soviet defeat was a warning about occupying the country that the US later relearned.",
 story="On 24 December 1979 Soviet forces entered Afghanistan; on 27 December Soviet special forces stormed the Tajbeg palace in Kabul, killed Afghan leader Hafizullah Amin and installed Babrak Karmal. Afghan mujahideen, armed through Pakistan by the United States, Saudi Arabia and China, fought them for nine years. About 15,000 Soviet soldiers died by the Soviet count; Afghan deaths are put at a million or more, and some 5 million Afghans fled to Pakistan and Iran.",
 why="Moscow feared losing a communist neighbour to rebellion and infighting. It distrusted Amin, who had killed his predecessor and was sounding out the West.",
 then="The war drew foreign Islamist volunteers, among them Osama bin Laden. Soviet troops left in February 1989; the Afghan communist government fell in 1992, civil war followed and the Taliban took Kabul in 1996.",
 fact="The United States led a boycott of the 1980 Moscow Olympics over the invasion, and some 65 countries stayed away."),
"sa-bhopal": dict(
 bluf="A Union Carbide pesticide plant in Bhopal, running at a loss with safety systems shut down, stood beside densely settled neighbourhoods. On the night of 2-3 December 1984 about 40 tonnes of methyl isocyanate escaped and killed about 3,800 people by the official immediate count, with later estimates of total deaths far higher, and injured hundreds of thousands. The world's worst industrial accident became the test case for holding multinational companies to account in poor countries, and the company settled for $470 million in 1989.",
 now="Bhopal produced the US Emergency Planning and Community Right-to-Know Act of 1986 and India's Environment Protection Act of 1986; the site remains contaminated and claims are still in litigation.",
 story="On the night of 2-3 December 1984 about 40 tonnes of methyl isocyanate gas escaped from a storage tank at the Union Carbide India Limited pesticide plant and spread over sleeping neighbourhoods. The official immediate death toll was about 3,800; later estimates of total deaths run to 15,000 or more, and hundreds of thousands were injured. Union Carbide chairman Warren Anderson was arrested on arrival in Bhopal and released on bail.",
 why="Safety systems at a loss-making plant, including the tank's refrigeration and the gas scrubber, had been shut down or neglected, and a dense neighbourhood had grown up around it. Union Carbide holds that a worker's sabotage put water in the tank; Indian investigators found that water entered through poor maintenance.",
 then="Union Carbide paid $470 million in a 1989 settlement with the Indian government that victims called inadequate. Anderson never returned to face Indian charges and died in 2014. Seven Indian managers were convicted of negligence in 2010 and sentenced to two years. Dow Chemical bought Union Carbide in 2001 and denies liability.",
 fact="The settlement came to about a seventh of the $3.3 billion India had sued for."),
"sa-indira": dict(
 bluf="Indian Prime Minister Indira Gandhi sent the army into the Golden Temple in June 1984 to remove armed Sikh militants under Jarnail Singh Bhindranwale, and two of her Sikh bodyguards killed her for it on 31 October 1984. Mobs then killed about 3,000 Sikhs in Delhi over three days while police stood by. The Punjab insurgency ran on into the 1990s, and the failure to punish the 1984 killings remains a grievance in Sikh politics in India and abroad.",
 now="Justice for the 1984 killings of Sikhs remains a political issue in India, and Sikh separatism is still a source of tension, including in India's relations with Canada.",
 story="In June 1984 Indian Prime Minister Indira Gandhi ordered Operation Blue Star, sending troops into the Golden Temple complex at Amritsar to remove armed militants led by Jarnail Singh Bhindranwale; he and hundreds of others died by the official count, more by Sikh accounts. On 31 October two of her Sikh bodyguards, Beant Singh and Satwant Singh, shot her in the garden of her residence. Over the next three days mobs killed about 3,000 Sikhs in Delhi, often working from lists of Sikh homes, with little police response.",
 why="The bodyguards sought revenge for the assault on Sikhism's holiest shrine. The Punjab crisis grew from Sikh demands for autonomy, and Congress politicians had earlier promoted Bhindranwale as a counter to the Akali Dal party.",
 then="Her son Rajiv Gandhi became prime minister and won a landslide in December 1984. The Punjab insurgency continued into the 1990s and killed thousands. Rajiv was assassinated in 1991 by a Tamil Tiger suicide bomber. Congress politician Sajjan Kumar was convicted for the 1984 killings only in 2018.",
 fact="Rajiv Gandhi's remark that 'when a big tree falls, the earth shakes' is still cited by Sikhs as excusing the killings."),
"d-stinger": dict(
 bluf="The CIA, pushed by Congress, set out to raise the cost of the Soviet war in Afghanistan by giving the mujahideen a weapon against helicopter gunships. Stinger missiles fired near Jalalabad in September 1986 brought down Soviet helicopters and forced Soviet aircraft to fly higher and less often. The war's cost to Moscow rose as Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev was deciding to leave, and the weapons and fighters the programme created shaped the Afghan wars that followed.",
 now="The flood of weapons and foreign volunteers shaped the Afghan wars that followed, and hundreds of unrecovered Stingers worried air forces for a decade.",
 story="Through Operation Cyclone the CIA channelled billions of dollars of weapons through Pakistan's Inter-Services Intelligence to Afghan rebels from 1979. On 25 September 1986 mujahideen fired the first Stingers near Jalalabad airfield and brought down Soviet helicopters. The United States supplied some 2,000 of the missiles over the rest of the war.",
 why="The United States wanted to make Afghanistan the Soviet Union's Vietnam. Congress, led by Representative Charlie Wilson, pushed a reluctant CIA to supply advanced missiles.",
 then="Soviet helicopter and transport losses mounted. One reading holds that the Stingers made the war unwinnable; the other holds that Gorbachev had told the Politburo in 1985 that he wanted out, before the first missile was fired. Soviet forces left in February 1989. Many Stingers were never recovered, and the CIA ran a buy-back programme in the 1990s.",
 fact="The CIA spent tens of millions of dollars in the 1990s buying back missiles it had given away."),
"d-sovwithdraw": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev set out to end a war he called a 'bleeding wound' and to free resources for reform at home. Under the Geneva Accords of April 1988 the 40th Army left in stages, and its commander General Boris Gromov walked across the Amu Darya bridge on 15 February 1989 as the last man out. The retreat weakened the Soviet state in its last years, and Afghanistan's communist government fell three years later to a civil war that ended in Taliban rule.",
 now="The defeat weakened the Soviet Union in its final years, and the bitterness of its veterans echoed in Russia's later wars.",
 story="Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev had sought a way out since 1985. The Geneva Accords signed on 14 April 1988 set a withdrawal, and Soviet forces left in two stages from May 1988. On 15 February 1989 their commander General Boris Gromov was the last to walk across the Friendship Bridge over the Amu Darya at Termez. About 15,000 Soviet soldiers had died in nine years.",
 why="Gorbachev called the war a 'bleeding wound' in 1986 and wanted to end its costs in money, men and standing abroad.",
 then="Afghan President Mohammad Najibullah's government held on with Soviet aid until the aid stopped in 1992 and fell that April. Civil war among the mujahideen destroyed much of Kabul, and the Taliban took the city in 1996 and hanged Najibullah.",
 fact="Gromov's teenage son met him in the middle of the bridge with a bunch of carnations."),
"sa-reforms1991": dict(
 bluf="Indian Prime Minister P. V. Narasimha Rao and Finance Minister Manmohan Singh set out to avert default on India's foreign debt and used the crisis to dismantle the 'licence raj'. The budget of July 1991 devalued the rupee, cut tariffs and scrapped most industrial licensing, with an IMF loan secured against pledged gold. Growth accelerated from the 1990s, hundreds of millions left extreme poverty and India became a power the United States and China both court.",
 now="India is now one of the world's largest economies, and its rise makes it a key swing power between the United States and China.",
 story="In mid-1991 India's foreign reserves covered about two weeks of imports after the Gulf War oil shock and the loss of Soviet trade. The government flew gold to London as collateral and turned to the IMF. Indian Prime Minister P. V. Narasimha Rao took office in June 1991; Finance Minister Manmohan Singh's budget of 24 July 1991 devalued the rupee, cut tariffs, scrapped most industrial licensing and opened sectors to foreign investment.",
 why="A balance-of-payments crisis, worsened by the Gulf War oil shock and the collapse of trade with the Soviet Union, came first. Four decades of licensing had held growth to about 3.5 per cent a year.",
 then="Growth rose above 6 per cent a year from the mid-1990s. India's IT and services industries grew into exporters, and hundreds of millions rose out of extreme poverty. Singh became prime minister in 2004.",
 fact="Singh closed the budget speech with Victor Hugo's line that no power on earth can stop an idea whose time has come."),
"sa-pokhran1998": dict(
 bluf="India's new BJP-led government under Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee set out to declare India a nuclear-weapon state, and Pakistan's government under Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif to match it. India fired five devices at Pokhran on 11 and 13 May 1998 and Pakistan six in the Chagai Hills within three weeks. South Asia was openly nuclear-armed, and deterrence has coexisted since with a limited war at Kargil in 1999 and strikes between the two in 2019 and 2025.",
 now="Two nuclear-armed states have fought a limited war and exchanged air strikes since 1998, which makes Kashmir the dispute most likely to involve nuclear weapons.",
 story="On 11 and 13 May 1998 India set off five nuclear devices at Pokhran in the Rajasthan desert, its first tests since 1974, two months after a BJP-led government took office under Indian Prime Minister Atal Bihari Vajpayee. Pakistan under Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif answered on 28 and 30 May with six tests in the Chagai Hills of Balochistan. Both declared themselves nuclear-weapon states; neither had signed the Non-Proliferation Treaty.",
 why="Rivalry with Pakistan over Kashmir came first, with India's concern about China's arsenal. The BJP had promised to exercise the nuclear option and saw nuclear status as a mark of great-power standing. Pakistan's government judged that it could not leave India's tests unanswered.",
 then="The United States and Japan imposed sanctions, lifted by 2001. In May to July 1999 the two fought a limited war at Kargil in Kashmir. India and the United States signed a civil nuclear agreement in 2008.",
 fact="US satellites missed the preparations at Pokhran, and the CIA director ordered a review of the intelligence failure."),
"as200173b": dict(
 bluf="The United States set out to destroy al-Qaeda's bases and remove the Taliban government that sheltered Osama bin Laden after the 11 September attacks. Air power and special forces backing the Northern Alliance drove the Taliban from Kabul by November 2001; bin Laden escaped into Pakistan. The war ran 20 years, NATO's longest, and the Taliban's return in 2021 made it the case cited for the limits of state-building by outside powers.",
 now="The Taliban returned to power in 2021 and have barred girls from secondary school and women from most work, while the chaotic US exit damaged American credibility.",
 story="After the Taliban refused to hand over Osama bin Laden, US and British air strikes began on 7 October 2001. US special forces and CIA teams backing the Northern Alliance took Mazar-i-Sharif on 9 November and Kabul on 13 November. Kandahar fell in December, and bin Laden escaped from the Tora Bora mountains into Pakistan that month. The Bonn conference in December installed Hamid Karzai at the head of an interim government.",
 why="The Taliban refused to hand over bin Laden after the 9/11 attacks, and the United States wanted to destroy al-Qaeda's bases.",
 then="The war lasted 20 years as a Taliban insurgency regrouped from Pakistan after 2005. US troops peaked at about 100,000 in 2010-11. The Taliban retook Kabul in August 2021.",
 fact="The Taliban offered in October 2001 to hand bin Laden to a third country if the United States stopped bombing and produced evidence; Washington refused."),
"na201194": dict(
 bluf="US President Barack Obama ordered a raid to kill or capture Osama bin Laden at a compound the CIA had traced through a courier, choosing a raid over an air strike so that the death could be confirmed. Navy SEALs flew from Afghanistan into Abbottabad on 2 May 2011 and killed him. Al-Qaeda lost its founder ten years after 9/11, and the raid on Pakistani soil, carried out without Pakistan's knowledge, deepened mistrust between Washington and Islamabad.",
 now="It closed a chapter of the post-9/11 era, and US attention shifted from counterterrorism toward rivalry with China and Russia.",
 story="CIA analysts tracked one of bin Laden's couriers to a walled compound in Abbottabad, near Pakistan's main military academy. On 2 May 2011 about two dozen US Navy SEALs flew in two helicopters from Jalalabad in Afghanistan, one of which crash-landed inside the compound. They killed bin Laden and four others and took his body, which was buried at sea from the USS Carl Vinson. Pakistan learned of the raid after the helicopters had left.",
 why="The courier lead came first. US President Barack Obama chose a raid over an air strike to confirm the death and limit civilian casualties.",
 then="Al-Qaeda's central leadership weakened under Ayman al-Zawahiri, though its regional branches and its rival ISIS survived. Pakistan's parliament condemned the violation of its sovereignty, and the United States asked how bin Laden had lived there for five years.",
 fact="A Pakistani IT consultant live-tweeted the raid without knowing what it was, complaining about a helicopter over Abbottabad at 1 am."),
"as202178": dict(
 bluf="US President Joe Biden set out to complete the withdrawal agreed with the Taliban in the Doha deal of February 2020 and end a 20-year war. The Afghan army, built around US air support and contractors, collapsed in days, and the Taliban entered Kabul on 15 August 2021 while the United States airlifted more than 120,000 people. The Taliban restored their rule and barred girls from education beyond primary school, and the exit damaged American credibility with its allies.",
 now="Afghanistan is the only country that bars girls from education beyond primary school, and Russia in 2025 became the first state to recognise the Taliban government.",
 story="Under the Doha agreement of February 2020, US forces were to leave by May 2021; US President Joe Biden set the date at 31 August 2021. The Taliban took provincial capitals from 6 August; Afghan President Ashraf Ghani fled on 15 August and the Taliban entered Kabul the same day. An airlift from Kabul airport evacuated more than 120,000 people by 30 August; a suicide bombing at the airport gate on 26 August killed 13 US service members and about 170 Afghans.",
 why="The United States withdrew under the 2020 deal. The Afghan army depended on US air support, contractors and pay that often failed to reach the ranks, and its units cut deals with the Taliban as the Americans left.",
 then="The Taliban restored their rule and banned girls from secondary school in March 2022 and from university that December. The economy contracted as aid stopped and the central bank's reserves abroad were frozen.",
 fact="The last US soldier out, Major General Chris Donahue, was photographed boarding a C-17 at Kabul on 30 August 2021."),
}
